# Git Workflow Rules

## General Git Principles

### Version Control Best Practices

- Use meaningful commit messages
- Make atomic commits
- Keep commits focused and small
- Use proper branching strategies
- Implement proper code review processes

### Repository Management

- Maintain clean repository history
- Use proper .gitignore files
- Handle large files appropriately
- Implement proper backup strategies
- Use proper repository organization

### Collaboration

- Use proper pull request workflows
- Implement proper code review
- Use proper conflict resolution
- Implement proper communication
- Use proper documentation

## Branching Strategy

### Main Branches

- `main` - Production-ready code
- `develop` - Integration branch for features
- `staging` - Pre-production testing
- `hotfix` - Emergency fixes for production

### Feature Branches

- Create feature branches from `develop`
- Use descriptive branch names
- Keep feature branches short-lived
- Implement proper feature branch cleanup
- Use proper branch naming conventions

### Release Branches

- Create release branches from `develop`
- Use semantic versioning
- Implement proper release tagging
- Handle release branch merging
- Implement proper release documentation

### Hotfix Branches

- Create hotfix branches from `main`
- Implement proper hotfix procedures
- Handle hotfix merging
- Implement proper hotfix testing
- Use proper hotfix documentation

## Commit Guidelines

### Commit Message Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

### Commit Types

- `feat` - New feature
- `fix` - Bug fix
- `docs` - Documentation changes
- `style` - Code style changes
- `refactor` - Code refactoring
- `test` - Test changes
- `chore` - Build/tooling changes

### Commit Message Rules

- Use imperative mood
- Keep first line under 50 characters
- Use proper capitalization
- Be descriptive and specific
- Reference issues when applicable

### Commit Content

- Make atomic commits
- Include only related changes
- Test before committing
- Use proper commit hooks
- Implement proper commit validation

